{"connection_id": "BC-7c8005a53e19f1bd4dd0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Christ in God's form", "note": "The hymn begins with Christ in God's form, establishing the high status whose subsequent self-emptying makes its relation to the commentary's unlowered owner deliberate rather than generic humility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7fdaea76be31a9e9cbd7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "The Lord takes a slave's form", "note": "The commentary locates humiliation in the servant and says the owner does not undergo humiliation; the Christian hymn says Christ takes a slave's form, a striking reversal in which the one confessed as Lord assumes the servant's station."}
{"connection_id": "BC-61b1540bc4cebb666b6d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "form of a slave", "note": "Christ takes the form of a slave; the verse turns the commentary's owned and humbled slave role into the role voluntarily assumed by the exalted figure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3afd1aa37fe5c3719172", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "The servant-form is expressed as humility and obedience", "note": "The commentary defines worship as the utmost humbling and links the servant with obedience; the hymn describes Christ humbling himself and becoming obedient to death, while assigning this path to the exalted figure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e17189157c2705cd4634", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "humbled obedience to death", "note": "The hymn explicitly says he humbled himself and became obedient unto death, matching the commentary's association of worship/service with abasement while reversing who undergoes it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b7971588cc8f69fd0ca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "God highly exalts the humbled one", "note": "God's exaltation follows the slave-form and humbling, giving the commentary's secondary movement from lowliness to honor a markedly different Christian sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-53c12bd1b5e60926ed13", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phlm.1.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "no longer a slave but a beloved brother", "note": "Paul asks that Onesimus be received no longer merely as a slave but as a beloved brother in the Lord, an explicit Christian re-description of an owned person's relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-076efab6766041f841de", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fear, glory, judgment, worship the creator", "note": "The angel announces judgment and commands worship of the maker of heaven, earth, sea, and springs, bringing creator-ownership and Day-of-judgment worship together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c47c55038829d9dd4e69", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.19.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fellow servant; worship God", "note": "The angel refuses John's prostration, identifies as a fellow servant, and commands worship of God; it sharply distinguishes servanthood from receiving worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c8aec1ac3e7fdca74904", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.19.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all God's servants praise him", "note": "A heavenly voice calls all God's servants, great and small, to praise him; it provides a direct apocalyptic equivalent to praise voiced by those who belong to God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c830cad6e351fc619533", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.12", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The dead judged before the throne", "note": "The commentary names God malik on the Day of Judgment; Revelation's throne scene opens books and judges the dead according to their deeds, a direct eschatological counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30719106064d6a23f486", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's servants worship at the throne", "note": "The commentary calls the servant the owned person who worships God; Revelation's final vision says God's servants worship him at his throne, joining servant identity, worship, and the divine household."}
{"connection_id": "BC-387e4bea85226a85bae1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's servants serve in the final city", "note": "In the city of God's throne and the Lamb's throne, the servants will serve him; the final setting gives an eschatological counterpart to universal divine service."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ca10a5d9932b44e6f3f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Servants see God's face", "note": "The commentary pictures the slave as belonging to the owner's house; Revelation promises God's servants access to his face and name, adding intimate presence to the household relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-079a1e9d560f1a943752", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.4.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Creation exists by God's will and worships him", "note": "The commentary grounds universal servitude in God's act of creating; heaven's worship in Revelation calls God creator of all things and says they exist by his will, directly linking creation and divine honor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd9ca471cfdb3ff977ea", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.4.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "direct praise to the creator", "note": "The heavenly elders say directly, “You are worthy,” because God created all things by his will; it closely joins praise, direct address, and creation-based sovereignty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf33c64fc22da643d94d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.5.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Every creature worships the throne and the Lamb", "note": "The commentary says all who are created come as servants to God; Revelation gives praise from every creature to the throne and the Lamb, extending the universal service image while including the Lamb alongside God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d7fddfa34385cb2a875", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.6.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sovereign Lord asked to judge", "note": "The martyrs address God as Sovereign Lord and ask when God will judge, bringing master-language and the delayed final judgment into a single direct prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c57a4a9cbbd47c845b1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.1", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bodies offered as living sacrifice and worship", "note": "Paul calls the presentation of embodied life to God a living sacrifice and rational worship, giving worship a concrete, whole-person form beyond a merely verbal confession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acfa31ec68bd38d60fbe", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.14.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "another person's household servant", "note": "A believer is called another's household servant who stands or falls before his own Lord; the verse makes mastership a reason not to usurp judgment over someone else's servant."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ced1501af4a8ab81f45", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.6.16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Obedience makes one a slave", "note": "The commentary links Dīn with obedience and the slave with being humbled; Paul says people become slaves to the one they obey, directly joining obedience to servile status."}
