{"connection_id": "BC-d5758b45cea781758c11", "ref": "1 Enoch 62:9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Earthly kings fall before the enthroned Son of Man", "note": "The commentary links sovereign rule to the humiliation of servants before their owner; Enoch depicts kings and powerful people falling before the enthroned Son of Man and seeking mercy, extending the image of universal subordination into Jewish apocalyptic judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3499d87744da6c8acdb2", "ref": "1QS 8:13-16", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "prepare the way through Torah study", "note": "The Community Rule applies Isaiah's wilderness-way language to a separated group preparing God's way by studying and doing Torah, an interpretive conversion of road-making into disciplined obedience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-122e0e481ceb0de4abd7", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Qiddushin 22b", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "The pierced ear recalls that Israel belongs to God", "note": "The commentary says the servant is God's by creation and describes human beings as enslaved by other people; this rabbinic interpretation of the permanent Hebrew slave's pierced ear says the ear heard that Israel belongs to God and should not become a slave to slaves, directly limiting human ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-082be324a2c37d8e5ab8", "ref": "Baruch 5:7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "level ground for Israel's safe walking", "note": "Baruch says God commands mountains lowered and valleys filled so Israel may walk safely on level ground, a Jewish expansion of Isaiah's road image into divinely prepared passage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f0c2587a934a246fff7", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "servants serving without reward", "note": "Antigonus instructs disciples to serve the master without serving for reward and to keep the fear of heaven; it probes how divine service can be freely offered rather than merely compelled."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1560c7ae3279c1d5c3cf", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "yoke of Torah releases other yokes", "note": "The mishnah says accepting the yoke of Torah removes the yoke of government and worldly burdens; it develops divine submission as release from rival subjections."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f938151f290027f8929f", "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 2:2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "yoke of heaven and commandments", "note": "Rabbi Joshua ben Korha explains the order of the Shema as first accepting the yoke of the kingdom of heaven and then the yoke of commandments; this gives a Jewish liturgical reading of willingly assumed divine obedience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6508c6ff8218b8ff3190", "ref": "Mishnah, Avot 1:3", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Serving God without seeking a reward", "note": "The commentary stresses worship as a servant's status and obedience, not merely a feeling; Antigonus teaches that servants should serve their master without serving for a reward, adding a motive-focused Jewish reading of divine service."}
