{"connection_id": "BC-c14ea94eebdf9d7c3453", "ref": "Sirach 33:24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Donkey's burden and the slave's work", "note": "The commentary moves from a camel subdued by tar to a humbled human slave; Sirach places fodder, stick, and burden for a donkey beside bread, discipline, and work for a slave, explicitly bringing animal control and human servitude together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-648211fa99a6ff98d20f", "ref": "Sirach 33:24", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey's burden and slave's work", "note": "Greek Sirach pairs fodder, stick, and burdens for a donkey with bread, discipline, and work for a slave, explicitly yoking animal taming and human subjection as the commentary does."}
{"connection_id": "BC-052fb8d56bf9e95ea390", "ref": "Sirach 33:25", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A master assigns a servant work and finds rest", "note": "The commentary describes a human owner using a slave's labor and calls the servant humbled by work; Sirach says setting the servant to work gives the master rest, making the labor relation explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf287999df59911e7fd7", "ref": "Sirach 33:26", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Yoke and harness bend a servant's neck", "note": "The commentary's common image is one who has been subdued; Sirach describes yoke and harness bending the neck and punishment for a bad servant, offering a concrete Jewish parallel for bodily subjection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1edfa29f62ca42a1adb1", "ref": "Sirach 33:27", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Work imposed to prevent idleness", "note": "The commentary explains the slave through work that lowers him; Sirach commands work for the servant and treats idleness as a danger, adding an ancient household ethic to the labor image."}

