{"connection_id": "BC-868232695fc35a8f474c", "ref": "Sirach 33:30", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "servant treated as brother", "note": "After its donkey-and-slave discipline imagery, Sirach tells the owner to treat a servant as a brother; the nearby ethical reversal complicates a reading of the owned person as only property."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e2b7edc31b6d7e3dd85", "ref": "WLC:1Chr.29.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all comes from God; gifts returned", "note": "David's praise says all things come from God and that Israel gives only what is already God's; it makes praise an acknowledgment that human possessions remain received goods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2dfff381f0d2100b6ca5", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.21", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Israel challenged to choose whom to follow", "note": "The commentary hears a first-person commitment to worship one alone; Elijah confronts Israel's divided allegiance between the Lord and Baal, placing exclusive service in a public contest over rival gods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-52b1fe50f1c917434930", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Exiles refuse royal demand to worship an image", "note": "The commentary's 1:5 makes worship exclusive despite human power; the Judeans refuse the king's command to worship his image, showing allegiance to God under an imperial test."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20bfec7d0f19f706d684", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.18", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "refusal to serve gods or worship the image", "note": "The Judean servants tell the king that they will neither serve his gods nor worship his golden image, a concrete refusal of a ruler's imposed cultic mastery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8d221b886386ce1f6e3", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic דינא for judgment", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic د ي ن and links it to humbling, enslavement, possession, and the Day of Judgment; Daniel's Aramaic cognate root names the court's judgment, directly matching the judgment sense while not supporting those other senses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bf09470def1acf55ce1a", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic د ي ن (source labels B001, B004), Aramaic דינא", "note": "Daniel's Aramaic heavenly court says that the dīn sat and books were opened; the cognate juridical word keeps the judgment sense of “Day of dīn” beside the commentary's developed obedience and ownership senses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b8d2ce8241f258efed8", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Dominion and service of the humanlike figure", "note": "The commentary joins malik sovereignty to the servant's worship; Daniel gives everlasting dominion to one like a son of man and says peoples and languages will serve him, a close image of universal service under a ruler with a distinct figure in the vision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b9fa8e067bf5c61f224", "ref": "WLC:Deut.15.15", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Release of a servant grounded in Israel's own slavery", "note": "The commentary develops the human owner and enslaved person's status; Deuteronomy requires a master to release a Hebrew servant generously because Israel remembers being enslaved in Egypt and redeemed by God, adding an ethical consequence to divine ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-754d2743f5d9b5d54930", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.36", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew דִּין for judging his people and servants", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic د ي ن and connects it to both the Day of Judgment and the servant's subjection; Deuteronomy uses the cognate Hebrew root for the Lord judging his people and showing compassion to his servants, pairing judgment with the servant relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-77bc83a599ee3ba02433", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Father who made and acquired you", "note": "The song calls God the Father who made, acquired, and established Israel, closely matching the commentary's claim that createdness establishes a prior divine belonging."}
{"connection_id": "BC-538b079a398b8d18d998", "ref": "WLC:Deut.5.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "servant rests “as you do”", "note": "The Decalogue's Sabbath law requires the male and female servant to rest as the owner rests; it turns the master-slave relation into an equality of rest before God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-109e58c6c0d9d9877a03", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עבד for exclusive service", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic ع ب د for worship and service and reads 1:5 as service to God alone; Deuteronomy uses the cognate Hebrew root עבד in its command to serve the Lord alone, giving a direct lexical and ethical counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1136614394b6259f6d58", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic إله/الله (source label ء ل ه B001), Hebrew אלהיך", "note": "The command to fear and serve “YHWH your God” uses Hebrew אלהיך from the same ʾ-l-h family as the commentary's ilāh/Allah label and directs exclusive service toward that God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b2fbf3ab067e67ad9dd", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God brings every deed into judgment", "note": "The closing maxim says God will bring every deed, hidden or open, into judgment, providing an explicit Hebrew Bible counterpart to the reckoning implied by Day of dīn."}
{"connection_id": "BC-36b6c079467191f5c0f7", "ref": "WLC:Exod.1.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Pharaoh's forced labor of Israel", "note": "The commentary's Moses scene contrasts being raised in Pharaoh's house with Pharaoh's enslavement of Israel; Exodus describes the same people forced into labor, giving the human domination behind that contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8948bd86ef9a37a6037b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.1.13", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع ب د (source labels B001-B006), Hebrew עבד", "note": "Egyptians force Israelites into harsh service; the Hebrew verb is from עבד, a genuine cognate of the commentary's Arabic ع ب د, and its context preserves the coerced labor side of the root."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfeae541d2fa1422128d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.1.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Harsh labor and servitude in Egypt", "note": "The commentary's account of Pharaoh enslaving Israel is matched by Exodus's description of bitter labor under Egyptian taskmasters; the narrative adds the grinding work through which human rule humbles its subjects."}
{"connection_id": "BC-962a224f32049e82b6b7", "ref": "WLC:Exod.14.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Israel released from serving Egypt", "note": "When Israel has gone, Egypt describes the loss as letting them go “from serving us”; the phrasing gives the Pharaoh-Israel bondage in the commentary a direct narrative counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2f08791a60a4f02befc", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses raised in Pharaoh's household", "note": "The commentary pairs Pharaoh's claim to have raised Moses with his enslavement of Israel; Exodus says Pharaoh's daughter raises Moses as her son in the palace, preserving the family-raising detail while locating it specifically with the daughter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-973114639c5c2a9dcbbd", "ref": "WLC:Exod.20.17", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "house, servants, ox, donkey, every possession", "note": "The anti-coveting command gathers a neighbor's house, male and female servants, ox, donkey, and other possessions into one household inventory, closely matching the commentary's owner-house-animal-slave tableau."}
{"connection_id": "BC-36a0da27e1842bae7f83", "ref": "WLC:Exod.20.2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Lord identifies himself as Israel's redeemer from slavery", "note": "The commentary grounds human belonging to God in creation and contrasts divine ownership with Pharaoh's human enslavement; the covenant begins by naming the Lord who brought Israel out of a slave-house, making exclusive allegiance a response to liberation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09a9482e0e4980726e2e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.20.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "No bowing to or serving other gods", "note": "The commentary reads 1:5 as exclusive worship of the one owner; this command prohibits bowing to other gods and serving them, specifying the covenantal acts excluded by that allegiance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-caa241b39e93e4e5327e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.20.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع ب د (source labels B001-B006), Hebrew עבד", "note": "The prohibition against bowing to or serving other gods uses Hebrew עבד; it gives the cognate root an explicit exclusive-worship setting rather than merely a lexical resemblance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c8ea41d35e46f771d8c", "ref": "WLC:Exod.21.2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "A Hebrew servant leaves after six years", "note": "The commentary describes humans as God's servants by creation, not by an initial choice; this law limits a human master's claim to six years and requires release in the seventh, distinguishing temporary human servitude from the creator's prior claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec48c0d35e40054619a1", "ref": "WLC:Exod.21.21", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "slave described as master's money", "note": "The assault law calls the slave the master's “money”; it directly witnesses the property vocabulary that the commentary places inside the master-slave relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a72b21c3357d5122767", "ref": "WLC:Exod.21.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "A slave chooses to remain with his master", "note": "The commentary says human servitude to God begins through creation rather than a slave's choice; this law has a Hebrew slave declare love for his master and refuse release, a concrete human bond chosen after the term of service."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a4028fdf6b6068b225b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.21.5", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "servant chooses master, wife, children, household", "note": "The Hebrew servant says that he loves his master, wife, and children and will not go free; this gives a legal counter-case in which continued belonging is explicitly voiced as a choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1922a39712adabba8f5f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.21.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "doorpost, pierced ear, permanent service", "note": "The master brings the willing servant to the doorpost and pierces his ear for lifelong service, a concrete ritualization of ownership and enduring service in the master's house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e22f0fe676a3fd0a4f21", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.12", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Sabbath rest for ox, donkey, servant", "note": "The Sabbath command joins the ox and donkey to the child of the slave woman and resident alien in receiving rest, placing a divine limit on the labor imposed upon both working animals and dependent people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d9cd9300a6e0707d502", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.23", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Israel released to serve God", "note": "The commentary contrasts human enslavement with worship due to God; here God demands that Pharaoh release Israel so the people can serve God, recasting the exodus as a transfer from Pharaoh's forced labor to divine service."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f45430d58591027eceb5", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.23", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עבד for service to God", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic ع ب د for both worship and human enslavement; Exodus uses the cognate Hebrew root עבד for Israel's service to God, showing the cultic use of the same Semitic service word in the story of release from forced labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d5df8dd8272cf79471d0", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.23", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "release my son to serve me", "note": "God tells Pharaoh to release Israel, called God's son, “that he may serve me”; Hebrew תעבדני shares the ʿbd root with naʿbudu and turns release from Pharaoh into service of God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3bd291e3ba412287f900", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.18.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Every life belongs to God in a judgment context", "note": "The commentary joins God's malik status to human servitude; Ezekiel declares every life God's and then speaks of individual accountability, connecting divine ownership with judgment rather than human possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a78bf528c3c08638b5ac", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.18.4", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all lives are God's", "note": "God declares that every life, parent and child alike, is God's; the assertion broadens the commentary's ownership image from a household slave to all human persons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-839bd0142fc217a9bd99", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.28", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human dominion over living creatures", "note": "The creation command tells humankind to subdue the earth and rule the animals, providing a creation account of the animal authority and use developed through the tamed camel and livestock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de1ea19692bf5fb0b328", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.14", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "enslaved people and divine judgment", "note": "God says that the nation whom Abraham's descendants serve will be judged; it joins Israel's forced service to judgment, the two poles carried by the commentary's slave language and Day of dīn."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c8e4edb5f33817ce6bd", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.10", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "master, servant, camels, goods in hand", "note": "Abraham's servant takes his master's camels, with “all the good things of his master” in his hand; the verse supplies a concrete owner-animal-servant tableau for the commentary's property and domestication image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-281a3fb8441ca590a159", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.34", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "a servant speaks for his master", "note": "The speaker identifies himself as Abraham's servant, a close narrative counterpart to the owned person speaking in the first person after the commentary's shift to “we worship you.”"}
{"connection_id": "BC-be5612977e5c9796b26c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.35", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "master's livestock, servants, camels, donkeys", "note": "The servant lists his master's wealth as animals, male and female servants, and camels and donkeys, making the commentary's linked possessions socially concrete."}

