{"connection_id": "BC-df47f43974aa3c8eb7c7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.39.6", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Joseph's master leaves his house and possessions in Joseph's hand", "note": "The commentary describes rabb as owner of a house and malik as what a hand possesses; Genesis gives Joseph authority over his master's house while Potiphar retains ownership, sharpening the distinction between a human master's trust and ownership."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b899935d54bceaf70487", "ref": "WLC:Gen.39.7", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "master's wife commands Joseph", "note": "The Egyptian master's wife orders Joseph to lie with her, the immediate biblical scene corresponding to the commentary's Joseph speaking to the woman in his Egyptian master's house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2d61913cbc284c31f92", "ref": "WLC:Gen.39.8", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "my master, house, withheld possession", "note": "Joseph calls the Egyptian “my master” and describes his control over the house and its goods, giving a precise human-master use alongside the commentary's discussion of rabbi as an owner."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f3803eeb20da2986e9d0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.39.9", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "human master bounded by sin against God", "note": "Joseph says his master has withheld nothing but his wife, yet refuses because the act would be a sin against God; the human householder's wide control is subordinated to a higher Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4ae68d15ed4d35174b4", "ref": "WLC:Gen.9.2", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "animals placed into human hand", "note": "After the flood, every animal is given into human hands; the phrase makes ownership/control of creatures a concrete parallel to the commentary's hand-held property and subdued animals."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2d6d44dc36c40cb7a6d", "ref": "WLC:Hos.10.11", "target": "sec2", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trained heifer, fair neck, yoke", "note": "Hosea depicts Ephraim as a trained heifer whose neck will be yoked for plowing, a close animal image for the commentary's domestication, submission, and forced labor."}
