text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Update File: /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s001/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.session-20261005/sec2/verdicts.jsonl
@@
 {"connection_id":"BC-5fdc478e582b22955ec9","ref":"SBLGNT:John.1.38","status":"rejected","reason":"The Greek only transliterates Rabbi and glosses it Teacher; it does not establish the proposed Arabic rabb cognacy or the householder sense.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.1.38"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-afc5eaeaece2c7b91f3d","ref":"SBLGNT:John.13.13","status":"accepted","reason":"Jesus accepts Teacher and Lord immediately before describing his act of washing feet.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d86cd6194af7f1d447c0","ref":"SBLGNT:John.13.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek explicitly has the Lord and Teacher wash the disciples' feet and command reciprocal service.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8f03402b480f837a8271","ref":"SBLGNT:John.13.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The lordly figure's lowly service is the precise Christian counter-scene; it need not imply the Qur'anic owner lowers himself.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-90ebfbc455cebc9a1888","ref":"SBLGNT:John.13.16","status":"rejected","reason":"The general maxim that a slave is not above the master adds no new inversion beyond the actual foot-washing in John 13:14.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.13.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0182d24c560a88c1d763","ref":"SBLGNT:John.15.15","status":"accepted","reason":"Jesus explicitly replaces the disciples' slave designation with friends because he discloses what he heard.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Gal.4.7","SBLGNT:Rom.8.15","SBLGNT:John.15.15","SBLGNT:John.8.35"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-88380de7f1c9a7e7ffdd","ref":"SBLGNT:John.15.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The explicit friends-not-slaves wording qualifies a merely servile account of intimacy in the master's house.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Gal.4.7","SBLGNT:Rom.8.15","SBLGNT:John.15.15","SBLGNT:John.8.35"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-116a5d51aa63cdb9145d","ref":"SBLGNT:John.8.35","status":"accepted","reason":"The slave's impermanence is contrasted with the son's lasting place in the house.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Gal.4.7","SBLGNT:Rom.8.15","SBLGNT:John.15.15","SBLGNT:John.8.35"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a2f7c47b366efdf09072","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.12.37","status":"accepted","reason":"The returning master seats and serves his watchful slaves; the inversion is explicit.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4da3649d735d80a39225","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.19","status":"rejected","reason":"The returning son asks to be a hired worker (misthios), not an owned slave; the scene is about filial restoration rather than created servitude.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a13742fa53a31f8cbcbf","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.22","status":"rejected","reason":"The father restores a son with robe and ring; the household shame is not the slave-owner relation or a reversal of slave property status.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.22"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ff8feecdec01428d96e8","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.17.10","status":"accepted","reason":"Disciples speak in first-person plural as slaves after doing what was commanded, a close form of voiced service.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.17.7","SBLGNT:Luke.17.10","SBLGNT:Luke.6.46"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-009"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d9bbf0b9081774a6bddd","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.17.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The wording describes obedience without a claim to extra merit; it complements the slave's spoken pledge.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.17.7","SBLGNT:Luke.17.10","SBLGNT:Luke.6.46"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-009"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2ccaa510c81ff7eb5119","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.17.7","status":"accepted","reason":"The field-working slave establishes the literal household scene from which Luke 17:10 draws its self-description.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.17.7","SBLGNT:Luke.17.10","SBLGNT:Luke.6.46"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-009"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a42495b2792f5139f6d1","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.3.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The Gospel quotation explicitly makes rough roads smooth and lowers mountains as a way is prepared.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Mark.1.3","SBLGNT:Luke.3.5"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ebbbd0b26096be473042","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.6.46","status":"accepted","reason":"The speaker questions calling him Lord without doing what he says, directly joining address and obedience.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.17.7","SBLGNT:Luke.17.10","SBLGNT:Luke.6.46"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-009"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-87d1c88e3211955b4949","ref":"SBLGNT:Mark.1.3","status":"accepted","reason":"Mark uses Isaiah's prepared Lord's way in his Gospel setting; the same road image is explicitly present.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Mark.1.3","SBLGNT:Luke.3.5"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7e4e37dc4b6ec81df58c","ref":"SBLGNT:Mark.10.42","status":"rejected","reason":"Gentile rulers' domination is a social practice, but the verse by itself has no owned servant or divine owner; the subsequent Jesus-as-servant scene is more pertinent.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Mark.10.42"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-017466197bb07e1a1e86","ref":"SBLGNT:Mark.10.43","status":"rejected","reason":"The Greek uses diakonos, an attendant or minister, not the owned doulos at issue; John 13:14 shows the higher figure's service concretely.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Mark.10.43"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a287a44856f294218608","ref":"SBLGNT:Mark.10.45","status":"rejected","reason":"The Son of Man serving is genuine, but its ransom clause introduces a distinct salvation theme while John 13:14 and Philippians 2:7 express the role inversion more directly here.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Mark.10.45"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7e7d01d918d56e92af2e","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.18.23","status":"accepted","reason":"The king's accounting with slaves is the explicit parable frame connecting ruler, slave and reckoning.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.23","SBLGNT:Matt.18.25","SBLGNT:Matt.18.26","SBLGNT:Matt.18.27","SBLGNT:Matt.25.14","SBLGNT:Matt.25.19"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ef202cc5e1a26bfea939","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.18.25","status":"accepted","reason":"The unpaid debtor is ordered sold with wife, children and possessions, making debt judgment a property scene.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.23","SBLGNT:Matt.18.25","SBLGNT:Matt.18.26","SBLGNT:Matt.18.27","SBLGNT:Matt.25.14","SBLGNT:Matt.25.19"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-87ccb947fa92c3366bca","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.18.26","status":"accepted","reason":"The slave falls before his lord and seeks patience over the debt within the same account-settling parable.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.23","SBLGNT:Matt.18.25","SBLGNT:Matt.18.26","SBLGNT:Matt.18.27","SBLGNT:Matt.25.14","SBLGNT:Matt.25.19"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3f1b1646ab56386c8835","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.18.27","status":"accepted","reason":"The master releases the slave and forgives the loan; the parable does not present permanent subjection as its outcome.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.23","SBLGNT:Matt.18.25","SBLGNT:Matt.18.26","SBLGNT:Matt.18.27","SBLGNT:Matt.25.14","SBLGNT:Matt.25.19"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e103b3d83fab5db53c65","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.20.27","status":"rejected","reason":"Greatness becoming others' slave is an instruction among disciples, not the divine-owner relation; John 13:14 supplies the enacted lordly service used in paragraph 14.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.20.27"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a1dbf8ef7d80278b18b6","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.20.28","status":"rejected","reason":"The service-and-ransom wording parallels Mark 10:45 almost exactly and adds no separate point to the foot-washing and servant-form evidence selected here.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.20.28"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4ee2cd9ad140f9a34125","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.21.5","status":"rejected","reason":"The gentle king riding an ass is a citation of Zechariah 9:9, but neither text depicts the divine owner lowering himself or the ass as a slave analogue.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.21.5"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7055ce9ffe071d757b69","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.23.8","status":"rejected","reason":"Rabbi here is a teaching title, and the injunction concerns sibling equality rather than ownership or worship of a human lord.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.23.8"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a76d1d1a10bef48d3c5e","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The lord entrusts his property to his own slaves; the return and accounting follow in verse 19.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.23","SBLGNT:Matt.18.25","SBLGNT:Matt.18.26","SBLGNT:Matt.18.27","SBLGNT:Matt.25.14","SBLGNT:Matt.25.19"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4182e1ea8ce4ffdcf1e2","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.19","status":"accepted","reason":"The returning master settles accounts with the slaves, explicitly joining property service and reckoning.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.18.23","SBLGNT:Matt.18.25","SBLGNT:Matt.18.26","SBLGNT:Matt.18.27","SBLGNT:Matt.25.14","SBLGNT:Matt.25.19"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5335cb8f86afe65d2a3c","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.31","status":"accepted","reason":"The Son of Man takes a glorious throne before the nations are judged; the Christian identity of the judge is retained.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rev.14.7","SBLGNT:Acts.17.31","SBLGNT:Rev.20.12","SBLGNT:Matt.25.31","SBLGNT:Matt.25.32","SBLGNT:Matt.25.34"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5cb284b69551146c2197","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.32","status":"accepted","reason":"All nations gather before the enthroned Son of Man and are separated; the verse provides universal judgment imagery.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rev.14.7","SBLGNT:Acts.17.31","SBLGNT:Rev.20.12","SBLGNT:Matt.25.31","SBLGNT:Matt.25.32","SBLGNT:Matt.25.34"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2fff98501586a3880622","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.34","status":"accepted","reason":"The judge calls himself king and grants a kingdom as inheritance; that is a Christian royal-judgment image.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rev.14.7","SBLGNT:Acts.17.31","SBLGNT:Rev.20.12","SBLGNT:Matt.25.31","SBLGNT:Matt.25.32","SBLGNT:Matt.25.34"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-cc40e4655941d9c88528","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.4.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek explicitly pairs worship of God with service to him alone.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.27.23","SBLGNT:Matt.4.10","SBLGNT:Matt.6.24"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-008"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-cb6f2600d4eb04978a5d","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.4.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The same explicit exclusive worship-and-service formula illuminates the first-person pledge in 1:5.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.27.23","SBLGNT:Matt.4.10","SBLGNT:Matt.6.24"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-008"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-70231cb1de180c937498","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.24","status":"accepted","reason":"The two-master impossibility makes exclusive allegiance to God concrete against Mammon.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.27.23","SBLGNT:Matt.4.10","SBLGNT:Matt.6.24"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-008"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3d560c10c8cd764dfa6c","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.24","status":"accepted","reason":"The maxim about God and Mammon directly addresses divided service, though Mammon is wealth rather than a human master.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.27.23","SBLGNT:Matt.4.10","SBLGNT:Matt.6.24"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-008"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7efc9282831c0cd1b974","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The universal knee bending follows the slave-form humiliation and recasts Isaiah's bodily submission around Jesus' name.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-12cf110327c86f4086b3","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The text directs every knee to Jesus' name; this is the hymn's Christological reapplication of the universal submission image.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-bc12203f797ddfbaeeec","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","status":"accepted","reason":"Every tongue confesses Jesus as Lord to the Father's glory, the stated Christian interpretation of universal submission.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-02af27bd15f710070e8b","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","status":"accepted","reason":"The confession of Jesus as Lord completes the servant-form/exaltation sequence and preserves its Christological distinction.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.2.6","SBLGNT:Phil.2.7","SBLGNT:Phil.2.8","SBLGNT:Phil.2.9","SBLGNT:Phil.2.10","SBLGNT:Phil.2.11","SBLGNT:John.13.13","SBLGNT:John.13.14","SBLGNT:Luke.12.37"],"annotations":["S001-INC-KRA-002"]}
*** End Patch"));

