{"connection_id":"BC-868232695fc35a8f474c","ref":"Sirach 33:30","status":"unavailable","reason":"Sirach 33:30 has no local original witness; the proposed brotherhood reversal cannot be verified.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":[],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2e2b7edc31b6d7e3dd85","ref":"WLC:1Chr.29.14","status":"accepted","reason":"David says all things come from God and gifts are returned from God's hand, expanding the possession image.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.24.1","WLC:Ps.50.10","WLC:1Chr.29.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-004"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2dfff381f0d2100b6ca5","ref":"WLC:1Kgs.18.21","status":"rejected","reason":"Elijah asks for a choice between YHWH and Baal but the people give no pledge; Joshua 24:24 supplies the collective voiced service needed in paragraph 12.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:1Kgs.18.21"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-52b1fe50f1c917434930","ref":"WLC:Dan.3.18","status":"accepted","reason":"The exiles explicitly refuse to serve the king's gods and worship the image, an enacted limit to rival service.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.24.15","WLC:Josh.24.24","WLC:Deut.6.13","WLC:Exod.20.5","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-20bfec7d0f19f706d684","ref":"WLC:Dan.3.18","status":"accepted","reason":"The Aramaic uses pelach and segid for refusal to serve and bow to the image; it is thematic, not a Hebrew ʿbd cognate proof.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.24.15","WLC:Josh.24.24","WLC:Deut.6.13","WLC:Exod.20.5","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-a8d221b886386ce1f6e3","ref":"WLC:Dan.7.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The Aramaic noun dina names the sitting court and opened books, confirming the juridical side of din.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.7.10","WLC:Deut.32.36","WLC:Prov.20.8"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-SYD-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-bf09470def1acf55ce1a","ref":"WLC:Dan.7.10","status":"accepted","reason":"The cognate Aramaic dina is present; neither enslavement nor ownership is stated by this noun.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.7.10","WLC:Deut.32.36","WLC:Prov.20.8"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-SYD-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3b8d2ce8241f258efed8","ref":"WLC:Dan.7.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The humanlike figure receives dominion and peoples serve him; the figure remains distinct from the Ancient of Days.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.33.22","WLC:Eccl.12.14","WLC:Dan.7.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-005"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7b9fa8e067bf5c61f224","ref":"WLC:Deut.15.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Verse 15 gives remembrance of Egyptian bondage as the reason; the release and generous provision are in 15:12-14, and Leviticus 25:42 makes the divine-ownership limit directly.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.15.15"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-754d2743f5d9b5d54930","ref":"WLC:Deut.32.36","status":"accepted","reason":"Hebrew yadin is an inflected form of din and the verse also mentions God's servants and compassion, despite lacking the exact noun form.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.7.10","WLC:Deut.32.36","WLC:Prov.20.8"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-SYD-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-77bc83a599ee3ba02433","ref":"WLC:Deut.32.6","status":"accepted","reason":"The song calls God Father, acquirer and maker of Israel; its national scope is retained in the annotation.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-538b079a398b8d18d998","ref":"WLC:Deut.5.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The male and female slaves are explicitly to rest like their human owner, a limit to imposed work.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Lev.25.42","WLC:Exod.21.2","WLC:Lev.25.43","WLC:Lev.25.46","WLC:Deut.5.14","WLC:Job.31.13","WLC:Job.31.15"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-011"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-109e58c6c0d9d9877a03","ref":"WLC:Deut.6.13","status":"accepted","reason":"The inflected taavod is from Hebrew ʿbd and commands service to YHWH; the cognate supports worship usage without proving a loan.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.24.15","WLC:Josh.24.24","WLC:Deut.6.13","WLC:Exod.20.5","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-1136614394b6259f6d58","ref":"WLC:Deut.6.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Elohekha means 'your God' but this line offers no distinct ilah/Allah sense beyond the service command; lexical descent is not established by the inflected form alone.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.6.13"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7b2fbf3ab067e67ad9dd","ref":"WLC:Eccl.12.14","status":"accepted","reason":"Every deed, including hidden acts, is brought into divine judgment; this is an explicit accounting motif.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.33.22","WLC:Eccl.12.14","WLC:Dan.7.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-005"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-36b6c079467191f5c0f7","ref":"WLC:Exod.1.13","status":"accepted","reason":"Egyptians force Israel into harsh labor in the shared Moses/Pharaoh narrative; the direct agent is Egypt rather than Pharaoh by name.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.13","WLC:Exod.1.14","WLC:Exod.2.9","WLC:Exod.2.10","WLC:Exod.4.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-8948bd86ef9a37a6037b","ref":"WLC:Exod.1.13","status":"accepted","reason":"The inflected Hebrew verb vayaavidu belongs to ʿbd, the Semitic service family, and in context means coercive labor.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.13","WLC:Exod.1.14","WLC:Exod.2.9","WLC:Exod.2.10","WLC:Exod.4.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-bfeae541d2fa1422128d","ref":"WLC:Exod.1.14","status":"accepted","reason":"The bitter mortar, brick and field labor details specify what Egyptian forced service involved.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.13","WLC:Exod.1.14","WLC:Exod.2.9","WLC:Exod.2.10","WLC:Exod.4.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-962a224f32049e82b6b7","ref":"WLC:Exod.14.5","status":"rejected","reason":"Egypt's complaint that Israel left its service reinforces Exodus 1:13-14's already cited forced-labor point without adding to Pharaoh's upbringing claim or God's demand.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.14.5"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b2f08791a60a4f02befc","ref":"WLC:Exod.2.10","status":"accepted","reason":"Moses becomes Pharaoh's daughter's son, but the adjacent verse says his mother nursed him; the candidate's daughter-raised wording is corrected.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.13","WLC:Exod.1.14","WLC:Exod.2.9","WLC:Exod.2.10","WLC:Exod.4.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-973114639c5c2a9dcbbd","ref":"WLC:Exod.20.17","status":"accepted","reason":"The command explicitly enumerates house, slaves, ox, ass and other possessions in one household inventory.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.17","WLC:Gen.24.10","WLC:Gen.24.35","WLC:Exod.21.21"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-36a0da27e1842bae7f83","ref":"WLC:Exod.20.2","status":"rejected","reason":"God's self-identification as Israel's liberator from a slave-house is relevant, but Exodus 4:23 expresses the release-for-divine-service relation directly in the Moses scene.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.2"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-09a9482e0e4980726e2e","ref":"WLC:Exod.20.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The command forbids bowing to and serving other gods; its service verb is an inflected ʿbd form.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.24.15","WLC:Josh.24.24","WLC:Deut.6.13","WLC:Exod.20.5","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-caa241b39e93e4e5327e","ref":"WLC:Exod.20.5","status":"accepted","reason":"Taavdem belongs to the ʿbd root and is used for prohibited cultic service to rivals, a real lexical counterpart to worship usage.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Josh.24.15","WLC:Josh.24.24","WLC:Deut.6.13","WLC:Exod.20.5","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-008"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3c8ea41d35e46f771d8c","ref":"WLC:Exod.21.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew servant works six years and leaves free in the seventh, directly limiting one form of human ownership.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Lev.25.42","WLC:Exod.21.2","WLC:Lev.25.43","WLC:Lev.25.46","WLC:Deut.5.14","WLC:Job.31.13","WLC:Job.31.15"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-011"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-ec48c0d35e40054619a1","ref":"WLC:Exod.21.21","status":"accepted","reason":"The assault law explicitly calls the slave the master's money, documenting property terminology in a legal setting.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.17","WLC:Gen.24.10","WLC:Gen.24.35","WLC:Exod.21.21"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3a72b21c3357d5122767","ref":"WLC:Exod.21.5","status":"accepted","reason":"A Hebrew slave explicitly voices love of his master and a wish not to leave; this is human legal service by subsequent choice.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-8a4028fdf6b6068b225b","ref":"WLC:Exod.21.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The servant's stated refusal to go free is an explicit chosen continuation, contrasted with creator-based belonging.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-1922a39712adabba8f5f","ref":"WLC:Exod.21.6","status":"rejected","reason":"The ear-piercing ritual follows the chosen continued service in 21:5, but its procedure adds no independent point to the created-versus-chosen belonging distinction.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.21.6"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e22f0fe676a3fd0a4f21","ref":"WLC:Exod.23.12","status":"rejected","reason":"This Sabbath law gives rest to ox, ass and maidservant's child, but Deuteronomy 5:14 adds the more direct owner/slave equality of rest used in paragraph 14.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.23.12"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3d9cd9300a6e0707d502","ref":"WLC:Exod.4.23","status":"accepted","reason":"God demands release of his son Israel so that he may serve God, joining liberation and divine service in the Exodus scene.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.13","WLC:Exod.1.14","WLC:Exod.2.9","WLC:Exod.2.10","WLC:Exod.4.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-f45430d58591027eceb5","ref":"WLC:Exod.4.23","status":"accepted","reason":"Veyaavdeni is an inflected ʿbd service form directed to God in a release-from-Pharaoh narrative.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.13","WLC:Exod.1.14","WLC:Exod.2.9","WLC:Exod.2.10","WLC:Exod.4.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-d5df8dd8272cf79471d0","ref":"WLC:Exod.4.23","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew says release 'my son' so he may serve me, not the exact proposed taavdeni spelling; the inflected root and sense are secure.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.13","WLC:Exod.1.14","WLC:Exod.2.9","WLC:Exod.2.10","WLC:Exod.4.23"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3bd291e3ba412287f900","ref":"WLC:Ezek.18.4","status":"accepted","reason":"God explicitly claims every soul, parent and child, before stating individual responsibility for sin.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-a78bf528c3c08638b5ac","ref":"WLC:Ezek.18.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The all-souls declaration broadens the ownership image past one household and is explicit in the Hebrew.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-839bd0142fc217a9bd99","ref":"WLC:Gen.1.28","status":"accepted","reason":"The creation mandate gives humanity rule over living creatures and subduing of the earth, grounding the animal-authority side.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.1.28","WLC:Gen.9.2","WLC:Job.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-de1ea19692bf5fb0b328","ref":"WLC:Gen.15.14","status":"rejected","reason":"The Abraham promise predicts judgment on the nation served by descendants but lacks Moses' household or Pharaoh's direct exchange; Exodus 1 and 4 supply the shared scene.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.15.14"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-6c8e4edb5f33817ce6bd","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.10","status":"accepted","reason":"Abraham's servant takes his master's camels and goods, concretizing owner, animal and servant in one scene.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.17","WLC:Gen.24.10","WLC:Gen.24.35","WLC:Exod.21.21"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-281a3fb8441ca590a159","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.34","status":"rejected","reason":"The servant identifies himself to strangers as Abraham's slave, not directly to his owner; Psalm 116:16 is the precise divine second-person prayer analogue.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.24.34"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-be5612977e5c9796b26c","ref":"WLC:Gen.24.35","status":"accepted","reason":"The servant lists his master's livestock, slaves, camels and asses in a single possession inventory.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.20.17","WLC:Gen.24.10","WLC:Gen.24.35","WLC:Exod.21.21"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-001"]}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id":"BC-7d7f90ca03fb8f651931","ref":"WLC:Gen.39.4","status":"accepted","reason":"Joseph serves the Egyptian official and receives supervision of his house and goods; Potiphar is identified in 39:1.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.39.1","WLC:Gen.39.4","WLC:Gen.39.6","WLC:Gen.39.7","WLC:Gen.39.8","WLC:Gen.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2d5300fa255ee1b849af","ref":"WLC:Gen.39.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The house and all the owner's goods are placed in Joseph's hand, matching the human-master setting.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.39.1","WLC:Gen.39.4","WLC:Gen.39.6","WLC:Gen.39.7","WLC:Gen.39.8","WLC:Gen.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-df47f43974aa3c8eb7c7","ref":"WLC:Gen.39.6","status":"accepted","reason":"The master leaves his property in Joseph's hand while still owning it, distinguishing entrusted control from ownership.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.39.1","WLC:Gen.39.4","WLC:Gen.39.6","WLC:Gen.39.7","WLC:Gen.39.8","WLC:Gen.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b899935d54bceaf70487","ref":"WLC:Gen.39.7","status":"accepted","reason":"The Egyptian master's wife speaks to Joseph, locating the subsequent 'my master' response in the corresponding scene.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.39.1","WLC:Gen.39.4","WLC:Gen.39.6","WLC:Gen.39.7","WLC:Gen.39.8","WLC:Gen.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-c2d61913cbc284c31f92","ref":"WLC:Gen.39.8","status":"accepted","reason":"Joseph explicitly calls the householder adoni, my master, and names the goods placed in his hand.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.39.1","WLC:Gen.39.4","WLC:Gen.39.6","WLC:Gen.39.7","WLC:Gen.39.8","WLC:Gen.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-f3803eeb20da2986e9d0","ref":"WLC:Gen.39.9","status":"accepted","reason":"Joseph refuses the wife's proposal as a sin against God despite his human master's sweeping delegation.","paragraphs":[14],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.39.1","WLC:Gen.39.4","WLC:Gen.39.6","WLC:Gen.39.7","WLC:Gen.39.8","WLC:Gen.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-PRL-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-c4ae68d15ed4d35174b4","ref":"WLC:Gen.9.2","status":"accepted","reason":"Animals are explicitly delivered into human hands after the flood, supporting the animal-control aspect.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.1.28","WLC:Gen.9.2","WLC:Job.39.9"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-d2d6d44dc36c40cb7a6d","ref":"WLC:Hos.10.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Ephraim is compared to a trained heifer being set to plow; the political allegory does not supply the owner/animal/person sequence more directly than Genesis 1:28 and Job 39:9.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Hos.10.11"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-caad2fecd26258ef8523","ref":"WLC:Isa.1.3","status":"accepted","reason":"The ox knows its owner and the ass its master's manger, then Israel's failure to know is rebuked.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.1.3","WLC:Mal.1.6"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-003"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-56431fb89f76f71591ba","ref":"WLC:Isa.33.22","status":"accepted","reason":"YHWH is explicitly Israel's judge, lawgiver and king, bringing the royal and judicial titles together.","paragraphs":[11],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.33.22","WLC:Eccl.12.14","WLC:Dan.7.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-005"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-81070e570668f72d3926","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.3","status":"accepted","reason":"The wilderness way is prepared and the highway straightened; the comparison is physical road-making, not an ʿbd-root equation.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.3","WLC:Isa.40.4"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-498529d4075cf2f2d697","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.3","status":"accepted","reason":"The road-preparation summons is explicit and immediately completed by the leveling in verse 4.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.3","WLC:Isa.40.4"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-45ffb949eb22bffaafe2","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.4","status":"accepted","reason":"Valleys rise and mountains sink to make uneven ground level, giving the physical terrain image.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.3","WLC:Isa.40.4"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e997b602af5bb11a1cc9","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The leveling and smoothing of rough ground is explicit; its road-making purpose comes from adjacent verse 3.","paragraphs":[9],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.3","WLC:Isa.40.4"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-850988179ecc2b0ba9bf","ref":"WLC:Isa.43.1","status":"accepted","reason":"The creator and former of Israel directly says 'you are mine'; the national addressee is kept explicit.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-427a9ee821f90ba82f9f","ref":"WLC:Isa.43.1","status":"accepted","reason":"Created/forming and 'you are mine' occur together, though redemption and naming also mediate belonging.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-817bd5c72f3c37cb2872","ref":"WLC:Isa.44.21","status":"accepted","reason":"God calls Israel my servant and says I formed you, exactly joining formation to service status.","paragraphs":[13],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.43.1","WLC:Isa.44.21","WLC:Deut.32.6","WLC:Ezek.18.4","WLC:Exod.21.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-009"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3e134e3d65f2191371e9","ref":"WLC:Isa.45.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Universal knee bending and oath are an eschatological submission scene, but the verse does not ground existing human servanthood in creation; Philippians 2 separately reuses this image.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.45.23"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2704f93e8fbc3a1617c8","ref":"WLC:Isa.45.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Every knee bends before God in this oath, but no slave, house or created-belonging term appears; Isaiah 44:21 is the direct formed-servant text.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.45.23"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-af58c39190fa53a0451e","ref":"WLC:Isa.52.13","status":"rejected","reason":"God's servant is indeed exalted, but the verse contains no honoured-householder or worshipped-person sense corresponding to the base's muʿabbad gloss.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.52.13"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-a08ccf2824f8338fb4a4","ref":"WLC:Isa.58.6","status":"rejected","reason":"The chosen fast breaks oppressive yokes, a broad worship ethic; Leviticus 25:42-43 states the human-slavery limits in the legal owner setting directly.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.58.6"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-ecca2ea39e30ddc4ed21","ref":"WLC:Isa.6.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The written Hebrew calls YHWH ha-melekh, the king; the search flag missed this inflected m-l-k form, which witnesses sovereignty rather than property law.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Zech.14.9","WLC:Isa.6.5"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-SYD-001"]}
