{"connection_id":"BC-98ffa6c4792940a57bb9","ref":"WLC:Ps.116.16","status":"accepted","reason":"The first-person servant address and release from bonds coexist in one prayer; the scope is the individual psalmist.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.116.16","WLC:Ps.123.2","WLC:Ps.95.6"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-007"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-5894d0fd39093cf61dc0","ref":"WLC:Ps.119.91","status":"rejected","reason":"All things stand under God's decrees as his servants, but this cosmic claim lacks the created human's spoken acceptance; Isaiah 44:21 gives formed human service directly.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.119.91"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-49469788d1fc591a8bdb","ref":"WLC:Ps.123.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The eyes of male and female servants toward masters' hands are explicitly compared with the community's gaze to God.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.116.16","WLC:Ps.123.2","WLC:Ps.95.6"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-007"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-198b0fdc3e4b36279fde","ref":"WLC:Ps.123.3","status":"rejected","reason":"The servant/master eye comparison is in Psalm 123:2; verse 3 asks for mercy and mentions scorn without that image.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.123.3"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-c1e72690b6a20c4808aa","ref":"WLC:Ps.23.4","status":"accepted","reason":"Verses 1-3 speak of the shepherd in third person and verse 4 turns to direct 'you', a formal analogue with a different image.","paragraphs":[12],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.23.1","WLC:Ps.23.2","WLC:Ps.23.3","WLC:Ps.23.4"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-006"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-0aad3d67d4343aa91566","ref":"WLC:Ps.24.1","status":"accepted","reason":"The earth, its fullness and its inhabitants are expressly assigned to YHWH, extending possession to the whole world.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.24.1","WLC:Ps.50.10","WLC:1Chr.29.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-004"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b865caf40f2c3b8a282b","ref":"WLC:Ps.24.1","status":"accepted","reason":"The same ownership formula gives a world-sized horizon but does not by itself define rabbi l-alamin lexically.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.24.1","WLC:Ps.50.10","WLC:1Chr.29.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-004"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-5813e4b84e3b90165c34","ref":"WLC:Ps.32.10","status":"rejected","reason":"WLC Psalm 32:10 speaks of sorrows and mercy; the horse/mule bit-and-bridle simile belongs to WLC Psalm 32:9.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.32.10"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-8206b83218ae4e9162dd","ref":"WLC:Ps.50.10","status":"accepted","reason":"God claims every forest animal and the beasts on a thousand hills, placing human-held livestock under prior divine ownership.","paragraphs":[10],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.24.1","WLC:Ps.50.10","WLC:1Chr.29.14"],"annotations":["S001-TEV-MTF-004"]}
